[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Schema Design Patterns in MongoDB"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"MongoDB's flexible schema allows for various design patterns to optimize data storage and retrieval. Choosing the right pattern depends on your application's specific needs and data characteristics. This tutorial explores two common patterns: Polymorphic and Attribute."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Polymorphic Schema Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Polymorphic schema pattern is useful when you need to store documents of different types within the same collection. Each document shares a common set of fields, but also has type-specific fields. This is achieved by adding a 'type' field to distinguish between the different document structures."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a scenario where you need to store information about different types of media (e.g., books, movies, songs) in a single 'media' collection."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"{ _id: ObjectId("..."), type: "book", title: "The Lord of the Rings", author: "J.R.R. Tolkien", pages: 1178 } 
{ _id: ObjectId("..."), type: "movie", title: "Inception", director: "Christopher Nolan", runtime: 148 } 
{ _id: ObjectId("..."), type: "song", title: "Bohemian Rhapsody", artist: "Queen", duration: 355 }"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the 'type' field differentiates between books, movies, and songs. Each document then contains fields specific to its type (e.g., 'pages' for books, 'runtime' for movies)."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Querying Polymorphic Data"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can query for specific types of documents using the 'type' field."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Find all books
db.media.find({ type: "book" })

// Find all movies directed by Christopher Nolan
db.media.find({ type: "movie", director: "Christopher Nolan" })"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"Attribute Schema Pattern (EAV)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Attribute schema pattern, also known as Entity-Attribute-Value (EAV), is useful when you have a large number of attributes that are not applicable to all entities, or when the attributes are highly dynamic and change frequently. This pattern stores data in a more normalized way, with each attribute-value pair stored as a separate document or within an array."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a product catalog where products can have varying attributes. Instead of having a fixed schema with many optional fields, you can use the Attribute pattern."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"{ _id: ObjectId("..."), entity: "product1", attributes: [ { name: "color", value: "red" }, { name: "size", value: "M" }, { name: "material", value: "cotton" } ] }
{ _id: ObjectId("..."), entity: "product2", attributes: [ { name: "weight", value: "1.2kg" }, { name: "dimensions", value: "10x5x20cm" } ] }"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, each product has an 'entity' field identifying the product, and an 'attributes' array containing attribute-value pairs. This allows you to add new attributes without modifying the schema."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Querying Attribute Data"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Querying EAV data can be more complex, as you need to search within the 'attributes' array."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"// Find products with color 'red'
db.products.find({ "attributes.name": "color", "attributes.value": "red" })

// Find products with size 'M'
db.products.find({ "attributes.name": "size", "attributes.value": "M" })"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Note that these queries might not be very performant without proper indexing. Consider creating indexes on the 'attributes.name' and 'attributes.value' fields."},{"order":22,"type":"heading","text":"Choosing the Right Pattern"},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Polymorphic: Use when you have a limited number of well-defined types with some shared fields and some type-specific fields. It's generally easier to query and manage than EAV when the schema is relatively stable."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Attribute (EAV): Use when you have a large number of attributes that are not applicable to all entities, or when the attributes are highly dynamic and change frequently. Be mindful of the increased complexity in querying and the potential performance implications."},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Ultimately, the best schema design pattern depends on your specific use case and data characteristics. Consider the trade-offs between flexibility, query performance, and maintainability when making your decision."}]
